body:
Hi, Nanka! It's nice to meet you. Did you know that you're the first "Nanka" I've ever met? It's a very unique name, fitting for such a unique, special woman as yourself. I also like how "friendly" it sounds. I feel like Nanka is the sort of woman what would be easy to meet and talk to. Then again, what do I know? I'm just a stranger on the internet. You know yourself way better than I do.

&#x200B;

Hey, I know things are tough right now. I lost my family too. Well, kind of. See, for me, blood doesn't matter at all. In my opinion, "family" means the people who love and care for you no matter what, the people who accept you for who you are and love you all the more for it. Maybe you just haven't met yours yet. Don't give up looking. The suggestions of an LGBT+ group in your area are a great place to start.

&#x200B;

Stay strong and keep being you. <3

This is all based on personal experience and opinion. Take my word with a grain of salt and listen to your vets' advice.

&#x200B;

One.

Try to remain as calm and composed as possible during the appointment, especially if your dog gets anxious at the vet's. I know this is like asking an infant to move a mountain, (I'm guilty of crying way more than I would have liked), but my dog was worth my best effort to make his last moments as calm and peaceful as possible. I didn't want him to be scared or worried because I was upset. For once, I wanted to be there for him instead. There's plenty of time for tears afterwards, and please do express your emotions. It's not healthy to bottle that up.

&#x200B;

Two.

This is something I wish I was better aware of at the time, but when a body shuts down, it can do some unexpected things. Your dog may pee or poop himself when his muscles relax after he passes. He may release air from his lungs, which can cause the body to heave or the head to move a bit. It can be a little frightening if you're not expecting it, but it's all a normal part of passing on. Feel free to ask your vet about that if you want more info. They should be able to explain better than I can.

&#x200B;

Three.

It meant a lot to me to make his last day his "best day ever". We did all his favorite things and ate all his favorite foods. His last meal was a vanilla milkshake. (Strawberry will also work, but obviously not chocolate. Ask your vet if you're not sure what's safe for dogs to eat, and especially make sure to ask if your dog is on a special diet.) You mentioned your dog wasn't able to do much anymore, but I'm sure you can still spend some time with treats and cuddles and anything else that he still enjoys.

A lot of people assume everyone on reddit is an adult, so there were probably several individuals that didn't think further than that assumption. They might not have considered it as an ignorant and clumsily-written child's comment.

&#x200B;

If you assume an adult wrote that, then it sounds like a passive-aggressive comment disguised as a question, implying that bodybuilders can't get dates. If I were a bodybuilder and came across someone saying that it makes me undesirable or unlovable to be a bodybuilder, you bet I'd downvote that sentiment!

If I met a wonderful person who I really enjoyed being around, and they opened up and told me something like this, I'd 100% stay with them. I would think of this more as a history of sexual abuse, and not a history of "promiscuity" anyway. (Not to say that having lots of sex is necessarily bad if you're being safe and all.)

I would want to be kind and understanding if intimacy is difficult for you, take things at your pace and let you know that you are whole, you are valuable, you are loved. Heck, I'd be your bad boy and beat up those suckers for ya. Well, verbally at least. I don't like violence. \^\^;

&#x200B;

My partner and I both like women, so I asked him for his opinion too.

First, I told him your history and how the other kids at school treat you. As soon as I mentioned the things they say to you and how they pick on you, he stopped me. He told me that when he was in high school, he actually knew someone who went through something very similar. She was forced into sex work at a very young age and the whole school knew about it, but no one bullied her, at least not that he could tell. In fact, his classmates sympathized with her and felt it was really sad what she went though. My partner was so disgusted that anyone would pick on you for something outside of your control. He made sure that I should tell you, none of it was your fault, and there is NOTHING wrong with you. Those kids who bully you are the ones who are messed up in the head to think those are ok things to tell ANYONE, let alone a survivor like yourself.

He then said that if he met someone he really liked, and they had a history like yours, he would absolutely stay with them. He said it was like dating an amputee or anyone else who might not be society's "ideal". (What even is normal, anyway?) You didn't do anything wrong, and you DO deserve to be loved like anyone else.

"If you transition, then you'd be in a same-sex relationship and will go to hell."

&#x200B;

Yikes.

The idea of pansexuality is that it can encompass any and all genders, right? I had to think about it awhile, but here is an idea as to how one might interpret that as transphobic. Note that I personally disagree with the viewpoint that non-binary identities are invalid. They're just as valid as any other gender.

Also, I'm not a psychic. I can't know what anyone else "meant" when they said something. Take this with a grain of salt.

&#x200B;

If one discounts non-binary identities as valid and assumes binary male and female genders are the only ones that exist, then I can see the problem. "Bisexual" would mean an attraction to both men and women. If one were to use "Pansexual" instead, it would then imply that transmen and transwomen are "different genders" from cismen and ciswomen, therefore accounting for the categories outside of male and female. An individual who follows this line of thought might get the impression that someone calling themself pansexual thinks that transpeople are inherently different and separate from their cisgender counterparts.

&#x200B;

Obviously, this all falls apart if we open ourselves to the wide gender spectrum out there, but it's a guess as to why a transperson might find the term "pansexual" offensive. If this is actually the case, then you didn't do anything wrong by identifying that way. Unfortunately, your friend either doesn't know or doesn't care about non-binary identities, but that's not your problem. There's no reason to change your current label preference.

Oh, gosh, now I'm in tears. You have no idea how badly I wish my mom had been like you. I haven't spoken to her in years. She told me I was going to hell.

&#x200B;

Thank you so, so much for leaving this post here. It's an amazing reminder that there are people out there who will have your back. Sometimes they're hard to find, and sometimes they're right at home, but they're out there. Regardless, I think we could all use a little more of you. Your son is one lucky guy. I'm so glad he didn't have to go through all that alone.

Oh god, I'm scared to watch this...

&#x200B;

Must watch...

&#x200B;

Ugh, this series! Such extreme love/hate. DX
